the old economy still dominates in North  Korea vast factories churning out basic  products like this copper cable maker in  Pyongyang the country exports some  commodities though far fewer now than  its biggest trading partner China has  banned most import in line with UN  sanctions it makes money by sending  laborers abroad it also relies heavily  on agriculture but in Pyongyang at least  there have been some notable changes new  buildings have sprung up there are more  cars on the road and there's a growing  choice of shops and restaurants we got  to ride in a city taxi operated by one  of the country's multiple government  controlled cab firms even the public  transport system has seen some minor  upgrades  we're inside the Pyongyang metro it's  big its grand there's lots of people  using it but they're still relying on  these very old German trains now they  have introduced DPRK made trains they  say that's a sign of the economic  development here  passengers can also now swipe in rather  than relying on paper tickets back on  the city streets there's evidence of a  more significant technological upgrade  Pyongyang's residents are avid  smartphone users and they can out choose  from a number of different brands we  visited this store selling the arirang  make supposedly produced here in North  Korea  the top model costs around $400 and  comes pre-loaded with an Android  operating system and a host of apps  including five versions of Angry Birds  but as is often the case in North Korea  things are not always what they seem  international calls are blocked on cell  phones here and the only surfer you can  do is on the government-controlled  intranet for bloomberg news I'm Tom  Mackenzie in Pyongyang
 

